#message 1 "Thornwall's gate stands open. A royal notice hangs beside the road; bread and a lit brass lantern wait on the bench. The hall lies north across the drawbridge, and the meadow road runs east. An alligator watches from the moat."
#message 2 "BY ORDER OF KING BRANNOC\nBring home the underwater Bell, the undying Ember and the wandering Map before our last candle fails.\nAny squire may accept the errand. Journey supplies are on the bench.\nThe notice has a turned corner."
#message 3 "Behind the turned corner is a note from the cook: 'Polish the lantern with your sleeve. The stove salamander likes a face it can admire.'"
#message 4 "Wenna signs beneath the royal seal. The king's errand is hers: find the Three Bright Things and bring them home."
#message 5 "Your name is already beneath the seal."
#message 6 "The loaf is yours for the journey. A heron calls from the meadow."
#message 7 "The bread is already in your satchel."
#message 8 "The brass lantern is warm. Its little flame holds steady."
#message 9 "You already carry the lantern."
#message 10 "The lantern's brass is dull, but its flame is sound. A loose sleeve would polish it nicely."
#message 11 "You polish the brass with your sleeve until your face appears in it."
#message 12 "The brass already shines. The flame needs no tending."
#message 13 "Take the lantern from the bench first."
#message 14 "The loaf smells of barley. It would make a good gift for a hungry bird."
#message 15 "The alligator floats very still. Its eyes follow your boots. The drawbridge is the safe way across."
#message 16 "The alligator turns toward you. Swimming here would put you straight into its jaws. You stay on the bank."
#message 17 "You leave the safe bank and swim toward the alligator. It reaches you before the bridge does."
#message 18 "The king is in the hall, across the drawbridge. His notice can commission you here."
#message 19 "The meadow road runs east. A heron waits beyond the rise."
#message 20 "The drawbridge is sound. Walk north between its rails to reach the hall."
#message 21 "The lantern is already lit. Keep its flame for the roadside candles."
#message 22 "The alligator has eaten. It is watching you out of professional interest. Save the bread for the heron."
#message 23 "The notice must stay for the other squires."
#message 24 "The heron's call comes from the meadow to the east."
#message 25 "The Bell, Ember and Map belong on the king's table. His notice names all three."
#message 26 "Read the royal notice, accept the errand, or take the bread and lantern. The hall is north across the bridge; the meadow road is east."
#message 27 "The bread would be better shared with someone hungry."
